1
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:10,533
Example A: the neuron from chapter 5, with its gradients still empty.

2
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:15,333
Which node's backward must run first? Think about who needs whose gradient.

3
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:21,133
The output o goes first: its own gradient is 1, and it feeds the rest.

4
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:28,533
By hand, each step hands a gradient to children, so the order matters.

5
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:33,667
That works, but we want the computer to find a safe order itself.

6
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:37,733
Each operation stores a closure: its own little backward rule.

7
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:45,533
Plus passes it on, times swaps the inputs, tanh uses one minus o squared.

8
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:50,467
Topological order: every node comes after all of its children.

9
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:55,600
Walk down from o. A node turns yellow when we step into it.

10
00:00:56,200 --> 00:01:04,333
It drops into the list only after all its children are in it.

11
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:09,800
Children first, output last. Now we flip the list around.

12
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:31,067
Backward now runs along the flipped list and fills every gradient.

13
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:36,200
Same answers as by hand: x1 gets minus 1.5 and w1 gets 1.0.

14
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:41,333
Wrong order: x1w1 runs before its parent has pushed a gradient to it.

15
00:01:41,933 --> 00:01:46,800
It pushes a zero into x1 and w1. Their true values need the order.

16
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,000
Example B: a diamond, where one node feeds two different nodes.

17
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:59,800
Here b and c do not depend on each other, so either may come first.

18
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:04,333
Predict: will the other valid order change the gradient of a?

19
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:18,800
First order: the engine's own topological list, run from the end.

20
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:30,600
Second order: b and c swapped. Same gradient for a, nothing changes.

21
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:44,600
Invalid order: c runs before d, so c's gradient is still zero.

22
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,400
Expert corner: how much work is the sort, and can it fail?

23
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,700
The sort visits every node once, so the cost is nodes plus edges.

24
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:08,900
Twice the nodes, twice the work: a straight line on the plot.

25
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:14,100
Now one long chain: 2000 additions in a row, sorted with recursion.

26
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,900
Python stops us at depth 1000: a RecursionError, with 2000 to go.

27
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:24,433
The fix is a new function with its own stack, so no deep recursion.

28
00:03:25,033 --> 00:03:29,567
It sorts all 4001 nodes, and backward then reaches x without any error.

29
00:03:30,167 --> 00:03:34,500
Real frameworks avoid deep recursion for exactly this reason.

